WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
<v Human Services Supervisor>I think my unit is pretty open. I tell people

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.240
<v Human Services Supervisor>all the time that they can bring me anything. But

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.360
<v Human Services Supervisor>a renewal notice problem reached me through the call line

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.480
<v Human Services Supervisor>last week, and someone on my team had noticed it

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.104
<v Human Services Supervisor>days earlier.

00:00:13.454 --> 00:00:16.916
<v Program Consultant>That pattern is common. In one interview study, 34 of

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:20.378
<v Program Consultant>40 employees said they had at least once felt unable

00:00:20.378 --> 00:00:23.840
<v Program Consultant>to raise something important. The top reasons were fear of

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.302
<v Program Consultant>looking like a complainer and fear of damaging a relationship.

00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:31.012
<v Human Services Supervisor>But I would never punish someone for raising a problem.

00:00:31.362 --> 00:00:34.677
<v Program Consultant>Most leaders would say that. The question staff ask is

00:00:34.677 --> 00:00:37.991
<v Program Consultant>different. What happened the last time someone spoke? Were they

00:00:37.991 --> 00:00:41.306
<v Program Consultant>thanked? Did the issue go anywhere? Did a senior colleague

00:00:41.306 --> 00:00:43.626
<v Program Consultant>brush it off while the supervisor watched?

00:00:43.976 --> 00:00:47.345
<v Human Services Supervisor>That last one stings. A team lead did dismiss a

00:00:47.345 --> 00:00:50.713
<v Human Services Supervisor>new worker's question a while back. I did not step

00:00:50.713 --> 00:00:53.408
<v Human Services Supervisor>in because I trusted the team lead's experience.

00:00:53.758 --> 00:00:57.961
<v Program Consultant>Status matters a lot here. A study of 23 neonatal

00:00:57.961 --> 00:01:02.164
<v Program Consultant>intensive care units found that physicians felt safer speaking up

00:01:02.164 --> 00:01:06.366
<v Program Consultant>than nurses, and nurses felt safer than respiratory therapists. Where

00:01:06.366 --> 00:01:10.569
<v Program Consultant>leaders actively invited input, that gap narrowed. The lowest-status staff

00:01:10.569 --> 00:01:11.830
<v Program Consultant>gained the most.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.332
<v Human Services Supervisor>So the new worker watched me side with the senior

00:01:15.332 --> 00:01:16.908
<v Human Services Supervisor>voice, and drew a conclusion.

00:01:17.258 --> 00:01:20.687
<v Program Consultant>Probably. The good news is that repair is concrete. Go

00:01:20.687 --> 00:01:24.117
<v Program Consultant>back, say they were right, and ask what else they

00:01:24.117 --> 00:01:27.546
<v Program Consultant>have noticed. Then build small habits. Ask for problems in

00:01:27.546 --> 00:01:30.975
<v Program Consultant>every huddle. Ask the newest people first. Report back on

00:01:30.975 --> 00:01:32.690
<v Program Consultant>what happened to each issue.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.971
<v Human Services Supervisor>Does safety mean I have to soften my standards? My

00:01:36.971 --> 00:01:40.903
<v Human Services Supervisor>unit handles eligibility. I cannot have people deciding rules are

00:01:40.903 --> 00:01:41.296
<v Human Services Supervisor>flexible.

00:01:41.646 --> 00:01:45.089
<v Program Consultant>No. In fact, a study of 126 teams found that

00:01:45.089 --> 00:01:48.532
<v Program Consultant>high safety can make it easier for a team to

00:01:48.532 --> 00:01:51.975
<v Program Consultant>agree on an unethical shortcut, when the team already leans

00:01:51.975 --> 00:01:55.418
<v Program Consultant>that way. Safety amplifies what a team values. The fix

00:01:55.418 --> 00:01:58.861
<v Program Consultant>is to make it safe to question a shortcut while

00:01:58.861 --> 00:02:00.582
<v Program Consultant>keeping the standard itself firm.

00:02:00.932 --> 00:02:04.678
<v Human Services Supervisor>So safety and standards work together. People can tell me

00:02:04.678 --> 00:02:08.424
<v Human Services Supervisor>the standard was missed without fearing blame, but the standard

00:02:08.424 --> 00:02:09.548
<v Human Services Supervisor>does not move.

00:02:09.898 --> 00:02:13.621
<v Program Consultant>Exactly. One caution: most of this research is correlational. It

00:02:13.621 --> 00:02:17.345
<v Program Consultant>shows these habits go with learning and better work, not

00:02:17.345 --> 00:02:21.068
<v Program Consultant>that they guarantee it. But they target the exact reasons

00:02:21.068 --> 00:02:22.930
<v Program Consultant>people give for staying quiet.
